WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.478
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B one.

2
00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:09.222
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.522 --> 00:00:16.621
Unit twelve: Question Forms (indirect questions). Today you will ask polite questions with statement word order.

4
00:00:16.921 --> 00:00:19.585
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:24.451
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:26.422
Could you tell me....

7
00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:30.461
Could you tell me... means a polite way to ask for information.

8
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.806
Could you tell me where the bank is?

9
00:00:33.417 --> 00:00:36.023
Your turn. Say: Could you tell me....

10
00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:39.961
Could you tell me....

11
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:41.602
I wonder....

12
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:45.626
I wonder... means a way to ask yourself or others politely.

13
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:47.763
I wonder what time it is.

14
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.095
Your turn. Say: I wonder....

15
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:54.934
I wonder....

16
00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:56.441
Do you know....

17
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.065
Do you know... means a common way to ask indirectly.

18
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.023
Do you know where he lives?

19
00:01:02.617 --> 00:01:04.770
Your turn. Say: Do you know....

20
00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:08.530
Do you know....

21
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:10.563
Polite question.

22
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.606
Polite question means a question that sounds respectful.

23
00:01:15.217 --> 00:01:17.356
A polite question avoids rudeness.

24
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.765
Your turn. Say: Polite question.

25
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:24.880
Polite question.

26
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:26.653
Indirect.

27
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.079
Indirect means not direct; gentler.

28
00:01:30.683 --> 00:01:32.648
An indirect question is gentler.

29
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.856
Your turn. Say: Indirect.

30
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.631
Indirect.

31
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:41.361
Word order.

32
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.162
Word order means the order of words in a sentence.

33
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.181
Word order matters.

34
00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:50.250
Your turn. Say: Word order.

35
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.171
Word order.

36
00:01:54.917 --> 00:01:56.173
Statement order.

37
00:01:57.183 --> 00:02:00.715
Statement order means subject before the verb, like a statement.

38
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:02.712
Use statement order.

39
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:06.132
Your turn. Say: Statement order.

40
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:10.432
Statement order.

41
00:02:11.183 --> 00:02:11.975
Formal.

42
00:02:12.983 --> 00:02:15.772
Formal means suitable for serious situations.

43
00:02:16.383 --> 00:02:18.336
A formal question is polite.

44
00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.393
Your turn. Say: Formal.

45
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:25.049
Formal.

46
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:29.287
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:33.673
Visal, how can you ask 'Where is the bank?' more politely?

48
00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:43.731
Could you tell me where the bank is? This is an indirect question. It begins with a polite phrase and uses statement word order after the question word.

49
00:02:44.817 --> 00:02:46.340
What is statement order?

50
00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:59.463
Subject before verb, no do or does: where the bank is, not where is the bank. Compare: What time does it start? Do you know what time it starts?

51
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.491
What about yes/no questions?

52
00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:17.314
We use if or whether: Do you know if she is here? I wonder whether it will rain. Remember there is no question mark after an embedded question when the introducing phrase is a statement such as I wonder.

53
00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:20.176
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:21.017 --> 00:03:25.954
Grammar focus: Indirect questions: polite phrase plus statement order.

55
00:03:26.254 --> 00:03:27.487
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:28.183 --> 00:03:29.916
Could you tell me where the bank is?

57
00:03:30.817 --> 00:03:32.712
Do you know what time it starts?

58
00:03:33.617 --> 00:03:35.024
I wonder if she is here.

59
00:03:35.967 --> 00:03:45.309
Use a polite phrase, then the question word with statement order: where the bank is. For yes/no questions use if or whether. No do / does in the embedded part.

60
00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.376
Be careful! Don't say: Do you know where is the bank?

61
00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:53.403
Say: Do you know where the bank is?

62
00:03:53.703 --> 00:03:55.191
Use statement order.

63
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.966
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:01.255
Polite Questions.

65
00:04:01.555 --> 00:04:04.159
Excuse me, could you tell me where the bank is?

66
00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:07.224
Do you know what time the shop opens?

67
00:04:08.233 --> 00:04:10.581
I wonder if the museum is open on Sunday.

68
00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:14.007
Indirect questions use statement order.

69
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.278
They sound more formal and more polite.

70
00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:22.508
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:24.943
Could you tell me where blank?

72
00:04:25.243 --> 00:04:30.278
A, is the station. B, the station is. C, does the station is.

73
00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:36.228
The answer is B: the station is.

74
00:04:37.383 --> 00:04:39.058
Do you know blank she lives?

75
00:04:39.358 --> 00:04:42.965
A, where. B, where does. C, is where.

76
00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.488
The answer is A: where.

77
00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.393
For yes/no indirect questions, we use blank.

78
00:04:52.693 --> 00:04:56.334
A, if or whether. B, do or does. C, what.

79
00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.097
The answer is A: if or whether.

80
00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:04.750
Which sentence is correct?

81
00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:11.525
A, Do you know what time does it start?. B, Do you know what time it starts?. C, Do you know what time starts it?.

82
00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:17.960
The answer is B: Do you know what time it starts?.

83
00:05:19.117 --> 00:05:22.637
According to the reading, how do indirect questions sound?

84
00:05:22.937 --> 00:05:27.183
A, more polite. B, more rude. C, more angry.

85
00:05:30.733 --> 00:05:32.919
The answer is A: more polite.

86
00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:37.552
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:38.417 --> 00:05:41.566
Rewrite a direct question as a polite indirect question.

88
00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.804
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:52.104 --> 00:06:04.372
Direct: Where is the library? Indirect: Could you tell me where the library is? I change the word order because after the question word I use the subject before the verb, as in a statement.

90
00:06:04.672 --> 00:06:06.811
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:07.717 --> 00:06:14.235
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and indirect questions: polite phrase plus statement order.

92
00:06:14.535 --> 00:06:18.348
Today's value is politeness. Say please and thank you.

93
00:06:18.648 --> 00:06:21.034
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:24.520
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

